diagnosis_text:
The CS department's report of intermittent "blinking" connectivity and a "failing router port" was investigated with a full Phase A → B → D sweep across all 30 lab devices. No fault was found at any layer.

**Affected component:** None — the CS gateway interface (router0.eth1, 192.168.2.1/24) and the switch1 uplink are operating normally.

**Mechanism (why there is no fault):** Every diagnostic check returned clean baselines. Carrier-change counters on router0.eth1, switch1.eth0, switch1.eth1, and cs_hod.eth0 all read exactly 2, which is the universal Kathara startup baseline — one down→up transition at container initialization, not evidence of repeated flapping. `dhcp_link_history` shows `network_down=0` on all CS hosts, confirming no "Network is down" journal events ever occurred. `pressure_sweep` found no flap script, no stress tool, and no runaway process on any of the 30 devices.

**Localization:** router0.eth1 (192.168.2.1) and the lan_cs segment were checked directly; cs_hod can ping its gateway at 0% loss and <1 ms RTT.

**Root cause:** No injected fault exists in this lab. The RIP routing table on router0 is complete and stable; all CS hosts have correct IPs (192.168.2.2–192.168.2.6) and a valid default route via 192.168.2.1; no ACL, TC shaper, link-flap script, or addressing anomaly is present anywhere on the CS path.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The reported symptom is unconfirmed — it has no active mechanism and leaves no historical trace in the lab. The ticket should be closed as unsubstantiated or escalated to end-user environment investigation (client NIC, upstream ISP, WLAN interference) rather than any change to the lab infrastructure.
=== END ===